- ✓ Explains reasoning for confidence
- ✓ Shows complete mathematical derivation
- ✓ Identifies this as the ""Birthday Problem""

## Accuracy Assessment

**Mathematical Correctness:**
The model's calculation is accurate:
- Correctly identifies the complement approach
- Properly sets up the formula: P(all different) = (365/365) × (364/365) × ... × (343/365)
- Arrives at P(all different) ≈ 0.4927
- Correctly computes P(at least one shared) = 1 - 0.4927 ≈ 0.5073 ≈ 50.73%

This matches the golden standard's result exactly.

**Assumptions Stated:**
- 365 days in a year
- Equal probability for each day
- Independence of birthdays
All standard and appropriate.

## Comparison with Golden Standard

**Similarities:**
- Both use the complement rule
- Both arrive at the same numerical answer (50.73%)
- Both explain the counterintuitive nature of the result
- Both provide high confidence levels

